# Line-ending policy - deliberately minimal.
#
# ONE RULE, for one concrete failure.
#
# The system documents under `context_compass/system_docs/` carry a BYTE-EXACT
# integrity gate: each `<name>_index.md` records the `content_sha256` of its
# `<name>.md`, and `_build_assets/_system_documents/_builder.py` refuses to
# ingest a document whose bytes do not hash to that value. That refusal is
# correct and intentional - "a range from an unverified index is a guess wearing
# a line number".
#
# Git stores those files LF. A Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`
# converts them to CRLF, which changes every byte-hash while leaving the LINE
# COUNT identical. The gate then refuses all four documents, `ingest()` reports
# nothing available, and the symptom appears far from the cause - as empty
# build-asset entries, with no error mentioning hashes or line endings.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting, so normalising the files alone does
# not hold: the next fresh clone re-breaks it. This attribute travels WITH the
# repository, which is why the fix belongs here rather than in anyone's local
# git config.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO:
#
#   No `* text=auto`, and no blanket `*.py eol=lf`. 439 of the 588 tracked
#   Python files under `src/` are currently CRLF on disk; pinning them would
#   rewrite all 439 on the next renormalisation - a repo-wide diff that fixes no
#   observed problem. Python does not care, and source-tree build fingerprints
#   canonicalize CRLF and lone CR to LF before hashing.
#
#   No rule for `_build_assets/_system_documents/payloads/*.py` either. Those
#   embed the documents verbatim and looked like they needed the same
#   protection, but the runtime gate was checked directly and PASSES as-is: the
#   document text lives in a Python string literal, so the file's own line
#   endings do not alter the embedded content.
#
# If a new hashed document is added under `system_docs/`, the glob covers it
# automatically. If a hashed artifact is ever added ELSEWHERE, it needs its own
# line here - the rule is scoped, not general.
#
# Renormalising an existing checkout is a one-time, repo-scale action and is NOT
# implied by this file:
#
#     git add --renormalize .

context_compass/system_docs/**/*.md text eol=lf

# LLM support bundles carry byte hashes and line-range indexes. Pin the whole
# generated/support lane so Windows and Linux produce identical proofs.
llm_support/** text eol=lf
